WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:09.860 --> 00:00:11.120
SCOTT ELLIS: Hi.

00:00:11.120 --> 00:00:12.920
Welcome to our
talk on how to take

00:00:12.920 --> 00:00:14.900
charge of your sensitive data.

00:00:14.900 --> 00:00:17.420
My name is Scott Ellis,
and I'm a Product Manager

00:00:17.420 --> 00:00:20.120
on Google Cloud's Data
Security and Privacy team.

00:00:23.560 --> 00:00:26.320
Now, data powers your
business, and data

00:00:26.320 --> 00:00:28.750
is one of your greatest assets.

00:00:28.750 --> 00:00:33.020
But it can also be one
of your biggest risks.

00:00:33.020 --> 00:00:34.940
And today we're going
to talk about how you

00:00:34.940 --> 00:00:37.130
can take charge of your data.

00:00:37.130 --> 00:00:39.080
And we're going to
cover topics like how

00:00:39.080 --> 00:00:42.440
you can discover and
classify your sensitive data.

00:00:42.440 --> 00:00:45.140
We feel that understanding
your sensitive data

00:00:45.140 --> 00:00:47.740
is the first step
in protecting it.

00:00:47.740 --> 00:00:49.740
And once you have that
kind of visibility,

00:00:49.740 --> 00:00:51.990
we're going to talk about
how you can manage your data

00:00:51.990 --> 00:00:55.790
risk using things
like security policy,

00:00:55.790 --> 00:01:00.570
confidential processing,
and data obfuscation.

00:01:00.570 --> 00:01:03.250
And ultimately, we want to
be able to do this at scale

00:01:03.250 --> 00:01:05.290
so that you can focus
on your business

00:01:05.290 --> 00:01:07.065
and, again, really
focus on the outcomes.

00:01:10.230 --> 00:01:13.410
Now, to do this,
automation is key.

00:01:13.410 --> 00:01:15.885
And again, if you want
to know your data,

00:01:15.885 --> 00:01:18.260
and if want to understand your
sensitive data so that you

00:01:18.260 --> 00:01:22.300
can better protect it, on-demand
scans and manual review

00:01:22.300 --> 00:01:24.020
can help.

00:01:24.020 --> 00:01:26.810
However, the challenge is
that your data is growing

00:01:26.810 --> 00:01:28.400
as your business is growing.

00:01:28.400 --> 00:01:30.620
And that means your data
footprint is growing,

00:01:30.620 --> 00:01:34.400
and it's more
challenging to manage it.

00:01:34.400 --> 00:01:36.880
And so you really need a way
to automatically discover

00:01:36.880 --> 00:01:38.200
and classify your data.

00:01:41.445 --> 00:01:43.070
And today we're going
to talk about how

00:01:43.070 --> 00:01:46.910
you can get automatic
discovery, automatic inspection,

00:01:46.910 --> 00:01:50.600
automatic classification, and
automatic data profiling--

00:01:50.600 --> 00:01:52.970
really, automatic DLP.

00:01:52.970 --> 00:01:55.460
And this is now
available for BigQuery,

00:01:55.460 --> 00:01:57.500
and it's powered by
our Cloud DLP platform.

00:02:00.242 --> 00:02:01.700
And what that means
is that you can

00:02:01.700 --> 00:02:04.340
get an understanding
of your data risk

00:02:04.340 --> 00:02:07.065
across your entire
BigQuery footprint.

00:02:07.065 --> 00:02:08.440
And that means
you can understand

00:02:08.440 --> 00:02:11.290
where you have, maybe,
low-risk data, which

00:02:11.290 --> 00:02:13.690
could mean that there's no
evidence of sensitive data

00:02:13.690 --> 00:02:16.990
or that it's got extra
protections in place,

00:02:16.990 --> 00:02:19.520
or where you have
moderate or high risk.

00:02:19.520 --> 00:02:22.450
This could be where you have
evidence of sensitive data,

00:02:22.450 --> 00:02:26.260
like PII, or evidence
of unstructured data

00:02:26.260 --> 00:02:30.310
or free text that might
be inside of your tables,

00:02:30.310 --> 00:02:32.890
or where you have highly
sensitive data, something

00:02:32.890 --> 00:02:36.220
like PII, like credit card
numbers, or financial data.

00:02:38.880 --> 00:02:42.870
And what automatic DLP will
do is generate rich insights

00:02:42.870 --> 00:02:45.403
for each table and
column across your org.

00:02:45.403 --> 00:02:47.070
And that's really
important, because you

00:02:47.070 --> 00:02:50.550
want to get that visibility
across all of your tables, all

00:02:50.550 --> 00:02:52.110
of your columns,
so you can really

00:02:52.110 --> 00:02:54.350
understand your data risk.

00:02:54.350 --> 00:02:56.990
Now, here's an example
of a column profile

00:02:56.990 --> 00:03:00.230
for a table where we're seeing
different types of metrics

00:03:00.230 --> 00:03:03.530
that indicate the type of
risk, the type of sensitivity.

00:03:03.530 --> 00:03:08.390
And it includes the
DLP's predicted infoType.

00:03:08.390 --> 00:03:10.460
And this gives an
indication in this table

00:03:10.460 --> 00:03:12.380
that we see evidence of
a credit card number,

00:03:12.380 --> 00:03:15.140
an email address, and a
couple of columns that

00:03:15.140 --> 00:03:17.005
have evidence of a person name.

00:03:17.005 --> 00:03:18.380
This kind of data
type can really

00:03:18.380 --> 00:03:21.410
help you understand your
data or take the next step

00:03:21.410 --> 00:03:24.700
to protecting it.

00:03:24.700 --> 00:03:27.310
Now, this automatic
visibility with DLP

00:03:27.310 --> 00:03:31.280
really means that you get
continuous monitoring.

00:03:31.280 --> 00:03:34.220
That means that tables are
picked up automatically.

00:03:34.220 --> 00:03:35.950
So as you're creating
new projects,

00:03:35.950 --> 00:03:40.460
new data sets, and new tables
in BigQuery, Automatic DLP

00:03:40.460 --> 00:03:44.150
will pick them up automatically
and profile them for you.

00:03:44.150 --> 00:03:46.460
And it's designed as a
fully managed service

00:03:46.460 --> 00:03:47.895
to have low overhead.

00:03:47.895 --> 00:03:49.520
What that means is
that there's no jobs

00:03:49.520 --> 00:03:53.180
to manage or orchestrate, and
you can enable this directly

00:03:53.180 --> 00:03:55.370
in the Cloud Console.

00:03:55.370 --> 00:03:58.580
It's also been built with
data residency in mind.

00:03:58.580 --> 00:04:01.340
And again, if you're turning
on across your entire org,

00:04:01.340 --> 00:04:03.360
you may have data in
different regions,

00:04:03.360 --> 00:04:04.970
different geographic regions.

00:04:04.970 --> 00:04:08.870
And DLP will inspect that
data in the geographic region

00:04:08.870 --> 00:04:12.500
that you have set for
your BigQuery tables.

00:04:12.500 --> 00:04:14.360
It will also
generate the profiles

00:04:14.360 --> 00:04:16.850
and store them in that
same geographic region.

00:04:16.850 --> 00:04:19.279
And that way, you can ensure
that your data stays where

00:04:19.279 --> 00:04:22.130
you want it, even when it's
being inspected and profiled

00:04:22.130 --> 00:04:24.330
by DLP.

00:04:24.330 --> 00:04:26.192
And lastly, this
is Google-driven.

00:04:26.192 --> 00:04:27.900
And what that means
is that we figure out

00:04:27.900 --> 00:04:30.420
how to inspect and
profile your data,

00:04:30.420 --> 00:04:34.110
so you can focus,
again, on the outcomes.

00:04:34.110 --> 00:04:34.610
Great.

00:04:34.610 --> 00:04:36.840
Now, let's see this in action.

00:04:36.840 --> 00:04:39.570
And now let's see a demo
of this automatic DLP.

00:04:39.570 --> 00:04:41.910
And for this, let's meet Angie.

00:04:41.910 --> 00:04:43.800
Angie is a data
compliance officer

00:04:43.800 --> 00:04:46.290
for a large online
retailer that serves

00:04:46.290 --> 00:04:48.600
customers across the globe.

00:04:48.600 --> 00:04:51.480
Angie oversees how data is
collected, shared, and used

00:04:51.480 --> 00:04:52.860
across all departments.

00:04:55.670 --> 00:04:57.680
Angie's company is
growing, and their data

00:04:57.680 --> 00:04:59.600
is growing with them.

00:04:59.600 --> 00:05:02.210
They've chosen to use BigQuery
as their data warehouse

00:05:02.210 --> 00:05:04.880
to power most of their
analytics and business needs.

00:05:04.880 --> 00:05:07.850
And usage of BigQuery is spread
across several departments

00:05:07.850 --> 00:05:09.410
and project teams.

00:05:09.410 --> 00:05:12.080
And again, these teams are
creating new projects, new data

00:05:12.080 --> 00:05:17.970
sets, new tables constantly
as part of their business.

00:05:17.970 --> 00:05:19.800
And Angie wants
to know, how can I

00:05:19.800 --> 00:05:22.770
gain visibility into the
sensitive data across my org?

00:05:25.410 --> 00:05:28.680
And for this, we're going
to use our Automatic DLP.

00:05:28.680 --> 00:05:30.360
What this means is,
you can now enable

00:05:30.360 --> 00:05:35.500
Cloud DLP across your org and
your entire BigQuery footprint.

00:05:35.500 --> 00:05:38.070
So what that means is
that you can turn DLP

00:05:38.070 --> 00:05:40.290
on across your
organization, as we're

00:05:40.290 --> 00:05:43.950
seeing here, across specific
organization folders--

00:05:43.950 --> 00:05:47.280
maybe these represent
departments or different parts

00:05:47.280 --> 00:05:49.080
of your organization--

00:05:49.080 --> 00:05:50.760
or individual projects.

00:05:50.760 --> 00:05:54.510
Now, BigQuery data lives inside
tables and data sets that

00:05:54.510 --> 00:05:56.430
reside inside these projects.

00:05:56.430 --> 00:05:58.780
So depending on where
you turn this on,

00:05:58.780 --> 00:06:01.380
you'll get coverage
across all that data.

00:06:01.380 --> 00:06:04.740
When this is enabled, Cloud
DLP will automatically

00:06:04.740 --> 00:06:07.980
pick up new data,
pick up existing data,

00:06:07.980 --> 00:06:10.980
profile it, and give
you those insights

00:06:10.980 --> 00:06:15.120
into your sensitive data.

00:06:15.120 --> 00:06:17.540
Now, enabling this is easy
and can be done completely

00:06:17.540 --> 00:06:19.910
in the Cloud Console UI.

00:06:19.910 --> 00:06:22.760
For this, at the
org level, Angie

00:06:22.760 --> 00:06:26.450
would navigate to Security
and then Data Loss Prevention

00:06:26.450 --> 00:06:29.338
and then click on the
Scan Org link here.

00:06:29.338 --> 00:06:31.380
Now, let's go ahead and
see what that looks like.

00:06:31.380 --> 00:06:34.130
I'm going to switch over
here to my test project.

00:06:34.130 --> 00:06:37.070
We're going to click
on Scan Organization.

00:06:37.070 --> 00:06:39.500
Now, we can configure
this in one click and take

00:06:39.500 --> 00:06:41.363
care of everything for you.

00:06:41.363 --> 00:06:43.280
But let's go ahead and
walk through the custom

00:06:43.280 --> 00:06:46.460
configuration, just so you can
see the options that you have.

00:06:46.460 --> 00:06:48.710
The first option
you have here is

00:06:48.710 --> 00:06:51.920
to choose whether you want to
scan your entire organization,

00:06:51.920 --> 00:06:53.900
or if you want to
browse and select

00:06:53.900 --> 00:06:55.910
individual folders to scan.

00:06:55.910 --> 00:06:59.340
For now, we'll just scan
our entire organization.

00:06:59.340 --> 00:07:02.000
Now, this service is
built with Cloud Data Loss

00:07:02.000 --> 00:07:03.500
Prevention, or Cloud DLP.

00:07:03.500 --> 00:07:07.640
So you can use your existing
inspection templates,

00:07:07.640 --> 00:07:09.120
or you can create a new one.

00:07:09.120 --> 00:07:13.160
But this is powered by the
same infoType detectors

00:07:13.160 --> 00:07:14.570
that we have for Cloud DLP.

00:07:14.570 --> 00:07:18.500
So you can select from
our built-in detectors

00:07:18.500 --> 00:07:22.430
or define custom
ones of your own.

00:07:22.430 --> 00:07:25.250
Additionally, you can
select the third option,

00:07:25.250 --> 00:07:31.390
which will run a set of our
most popular default detectors.

00:07:31.390 --> 00:07:32.890
And just simply
select that option.

00:07:35.840 --> 00:07:38.780
And next, you can
see the permissions

00:07:38.780 --> 00:07:40.160
needed to run this scan.

00:07:40.160 --> 00:07:42.350
Now, we're very transparent
about the permissions

00:07:42.350 --> 00:07:43.430
that are needed here.

00:07:43.430 --> 00:07:45.620
We can also create
those for you,

00:07:45.620 --> 00:07:48.590
or you can set those permissions
individually-- again, just

00:07:48.590 --> 00:07:52.650
depending on what is best
for your organization.

00:07:52.650 --> 00:07:55.350
And next, let's talk a little
bit about data residency.

00:07:55.350 --> 00:07:58.230
So data residency is
crucial to your data.

00:07:58.230 --> 00:08:02.700
And we've built this Cloud
DLP from the ground up

00:08:02.700 --> 00:08:04.440
to support data residency.

00:08:04.440 --> 00:08:07.680
And that holds true for
this automatic solution

00:08:07.680 --> 00:08:09.570
for BigQuery.

00:08:09.570 --> 00:08:11.760
What that means is
that when you have

00:08:11.760 --> 00:08:14.070
data in a certain
geographic region,

00:08:14.070 --> 00:08:17.190
configured in BigQuery to be
in a certain geographic region,

00:08:17.190 --> 00:08:20.370
DLP will scan data
in that same region.

00:08:20.370 --> 00:08:22.440
So the process of
scanning your data

00:08:22.440 --> 00:08:24.900
happens in the same
geographic region.

00:08:24.900 --> 00:08:28.590
Additionally, the
profile output will

00:08:28.590 --> 00:08:32.140
be stored in the same geographic
region as your BigQuery data.

00:08:32.140 --> 00:08:35.610
And again, this allows you to
maintain that data residency.

00:08:35.610 --> 00:08:37.200
Additionally, we
give you the control

00:08:37.200 --> 00:08:41.220
to decide where to store
the actual configuration

00:08:41.220 --> 00:08:43.740
data, which is what
we're configuring here.

00:08:43.740 --> 00:08:46.020
Again, we want to make
sure you have good insight

00:08:46.020 --> 00:08:48.930
and control of your data
to know where and how it's

00:08:48.930 --> 00:08:51.090
being processed.

00:08:51.090 --> 00:08:52.560
Now, if I click
Create here, this

00:08:52.560 --> 00:08:55.470
is going to enable
this new service

00:08:55.470 --> 00:08:58.587
for my entire organization,
which is great.

00:08:58.587 --> 00:08:59.920
However, I've already done that.

00:08:59.920 --> 00:09:01.670
So I'm going to go
ahead and click Cancel.

00:09:01.670 --> 00:09:03.990
And let's take a look at
what the results look like.

00:09:03.990 --> 00:09:05.830
Now, in this particular
organization,

00:09:05.830 --> 00:09:09.300
I only have these
six projects here.

00:09:09.300 --> 00:09:13.200
And each of these projects
has BigQuery data, data sets,

00:09:13.200 --> 00:09:15.610
and tables inside them.

00:09:15.610 --> 00:09:17.160
Now, here at the
Project view, I'm

00:09:17.160 --> 00:09:19.860
just getting a high-level
view of the tables

00:09:19.860 --> 00:09:22.750
and a summary of the risk
and sensitivity in them.

00:09:22.750 --> 00:09:25.630
Let's go ahead and click
on one of these projects.

00:09:25.630 --> 00:09:28.440
And now what I'm seeing is
every data set and table that

00:09:28.440 --> 00:09:32.460
is in that project and the
associated metrics at the table

00:09:32.460 --> 00:09:35.490
level, including things like
our data risk assessments,

00:09:35.490 --> 00:09:38.730
whether this data is public,
what kind of encryption it has,

00:09:38.730 --> 00:09:40.020
row count.

00:09:40.020 --> 00:09:42.390
And I can also click
on one of these tables

00:09:42.390 --> 00:09:43.560
to go even further.

00:09:43.560 --> 00:09:45.840
I'm going to click
on this table,

00:09:45.840 --> 00:09:48.690
and this will take me into
the column-level profile.

00:09:48.690 --> 00:09:53.080
Here we see detailed metrics
for every column in that table,

00:09:53.080 --> 00:09:56.490
including DLP's predicted
info type, which,

00:09:56.490 --> 00:09:59.070
again is-- if we can
figure out this column

00:09:59.070 --> 00:10:00.810
looks like a
particular data type,

00:10:00.810 --> 00:10:03.930
we actually label that
column here with that type.

00:10:03.930 --> 00:10:07.920
So here we're seeing a column
with credit card numbers, email

00:10:07.920 --> 00:10:10.170
addresses, and person name.

00:10:10.170 --> 00:10:12.930
It looks like we have a
first name and a last name,

00:10:12.930 --> 00:10:16.590
as well as metrics
around that for data risk

00:10:16.590 --> 00:10:18.835
sensitivity and some
additional metrics

00:10:18.835 --> 00:10:20.460
around whether or
not this column looks

00:10:20.460 --> 00:10:23.760
like it has free text, or
it's unstructured, maybe

00:10:23.760 --> 00:10:26.400
a comment field, as
well as uniqueness

00:10:26.400 --> 00:10:28.350
and null-percent estimates.

00:10:28.350 --> 00:10:30.780
Now, these are important,
because imagine this column

00:10:30.780 --> 00:10:32.010
of credit card numbers--

00:10:32.010 --> 00:10:35.040
we also see that there is
a uniqueness score of 1.

00:10:35.040 --> 00:10:37.350
And that's a high
uniqueness score,

00:10:37.350 --> 00:10:41.250
which indicates that this column
likely contains unique credit

00:10:41.250 --> 00:10:44.202
card numbers in every
row, which would

00:10:44.202 --> 00:10:46.160
be different than a low
uniqueness score, where

00:10:46.160 --> 00:10:48.560
maybe that same
value was repeated

00:10:48.560 --> 00:10:50.390
over and over multiple times.

00:10:50.390 --> 00:10:52.988
We also see that with an
estimated null percent of 0%.

00:10:52.988 --> 00:10:55.280
That means that this column
looks like it's pretty well

00:10:55.280 --> 00:10:58.520
populated, and there's not a lot
of null or sparsely populated

00:10:58.520 --> 00:10:59.747
data in these columns.

00:10:59.747 --> 00:11:01.580
But again, this is the
kind of rich insights

00:11:01.580 --> 00:11:05.450
that you get with the
column-level profile,

00:11:05.450 --> 00:11:07.910
as well as the table- and
project-level profiles.

00:11:07.910 --> 00:11:09.830
And all of this, again,
will be continuously

00:11:09.830 --> 00:11:12.320
running and generating
as new tables are

00:11:12.320 --> 00:11:14.285
created across your org.

00:11:17.500 --> 00:11:18.010
Great.

00:11:18.010 --> 00:11:19.660
Let's go back to Angie.

00:11:19.660 --> 00:11:22.810
Now, Angie needed an
automated solution.

00:11:22.810 --> 00:11:25.450
And Angie chose to use
Cloud DLP's new service

00:11:25.450 --> 00:11:29.320
to automatically discover,
inspect, and profile

00:11:29.320 --> 00:11:32.170
their BigQuery tables for
sensitive data like PII.

00:11:32.170 --> 00:11:35.530
And this automatic DLP service
will pick up new projects,

00:11:35.530 --> 00:11:38.650
data sets, and
tables automatically.

00:11:38.650 --> 00:11:42.940
And the outcome is that Angie's
team now has clear visibility

00:11:42.940 --> 00:11:44.770
into their sensitive
data and can

00:11:44.770 --> 00:11:47.380
make more informed decisions
about how it's protected,

00:11:47.380 --> 00:11:50.380
used, processed, and shared.

00:11:50.380 --> 00:11:53.080
And that concludes our demo
of the new automatic DLP

00:11:53.080 --> 00:11:55.050
for BigQuery.

00:11:55.050 --> 00:11:55.680
Great.

00:11:55.680 --> 00:11:59.160
We've now just shown you how
you can get org-wide visibility

00:11:59.160 --> 00:12:01.500
with the new automatic DLP.

00:12:01.500 --> 00:12:04.320
And again, this can give
you clear visibility

00:12:04.320 --> 00:12:06.300
into your sensitive
data so that you can

00:12:06.300 --> 00:12:08.830
make more informed decisions.

00:12:08.830 --> 00:12:12.840
And that's now
available for BigQuery.

00:12:12.840 --> 00:12:16.180
Now that you have that clear
visibility into your data,

00:12:16.180 --> 00:12:18.900
let's figure out some ways
to manage your data risk

00:12:18.900 --> 00:12:20.800
and reduce your data risk.

00:12:20.800 --> 00:12:24.000
So first, how can I enable
more fine-grained access

00:12:24.000 --> 00:12:27.540
control and protection?

00:12:27.540 --> 00:12:29.750
So now that you know where
your sensitive data is,

00:12:29.750 --> 00:12:33.710
maybe you want to go in and
employ column-level access

00:12:33.710 --> 00:12:35.060
policy.

00:12:35.060 --> 00:12:37.190
And using BigQuery
policy tags, you

00:12:37.190 --> 00:12:39.800
can enable column-level
access enforcement,

00:12:39.800 --> 00:12:43.250
which can help you strike
a balance between access

00:12:43.250 --> 00:12:44.450
and security.

00:12:44.450 --> 00:12:47.270
So an example policy
is shown here.

00:12:47.270 --> 00:12:50.390
And these policies have
a taxonomy or a hierarchy

00:12:50.390 --> 00:12:50.927
to them.

00:12:50.927 --> 00:12:53.510
What that means is that we can
put an element like credit card

00:12:53.510 --> 00:12:56.900
number underneath or inside
of a higher-level policy

00:12:56.900 --> 00:12:58.190
like financial.

00:12:58.190 --> 00:13:01.070
And that allows you to grant
access and manage your policy

00:13:01.070 --> 00:13:03.470
at that higher
level, while enabling

00:13:03.470 --> 00:13:08.370
you to tag the data at the lower
level, at credit card number.

00:13:08.370 --> 00:13:10.490
Let's see this in action.

00:13:10.490 --> 00:13:13.290
So here's an example
here where we're

00:13:13.290 --> 00:13:17.160
seeing a table where we have
tagged four of the columns

00:13:17.160 --> 00:13:20.580
in here with a tag for person
name, email address, and credit

00:13:20.580 --> 00:13:21.720
card number.

00:13:21.720 --> 00:13:23.610
And we have turned
on enforcement

00:13:23.610 --> 00:13:26.400
of a policy using
BigQuery policy tags,

00:13:26.400 --> 00:13:29.940
so that users can only access
those sensitive elements

00:13:29.940 --> 00:13:31.890
if they have the
right permission.

00:13:31.890 --> 00:13:34.960
And what that means is, if
I have access to this table,

00:13:34.960 --> 00:13:38.100
but I don't have access to
those particular columns,

00:13:38.100 --> 00:13:40.710
I'll still be able to query
the table and, in this case,

00:13:40.710 --> 00:13:43.830
maybe run analysis
on the payment type

00:13:43.830 --> 00:13:45.510
and the transaction amount.

00:13:45.510 --> 00:13:48.780
But I won't be able to
access the sensitive elements

00:13:48.780 --> 00:13:51.300
like the PII here that
we see unless I have

00:13:51.300 --> 00:13:53.268
that additional access control.

00:13:53.268 --> 00:13:54.810
And again that's
the way you can find

00:13:54.810 --> 00:13:58.320
that balance between
access and security

00:13:58.320 --> 00:14:03.320
and, overall, reduce and
manage some of your data risk.

00:14:03.320 --> 00:14:07.520
Another technique is to go in
and use obfuscation or masking.

00:14:07.520 --> 00:14:10.340
And this is something that
you can do with Cloud DLP,

00:14:10.340 --> 00:14:13.760
and it's a technique we
call de-identification.

00:14:13.760 --> 00:14:17.240
Now, de-identification
includes a set of transforms

00:14:17.240 --> 00:14:21.050
like redaction,
masking, tokenization,

00:14:21.050 --> 00:14:24.530
format-preserving encryption,
date shifting, bucketing,

00:14:24.530 --> 00:14:25.830
and more.

00:14:25.830 --> 00:14:27.920
And these transformations
are designed

00:14:27.920 --> 00:14:31.430
to actually manipulate
and transform your data

00:14:31.430 --> 00:14:33.720
in order to reduce the risk.

00:14:33.720 --> 00:14:37.580
So for example, in this
before-and-after animation that

00:14:37.580 --> 00:14:40.940
we're seeing, we're seeing these
techniques applied to a table--

00:14:40.940 --> 00:14:43.520
in this case, a table
that has four columns.

00:14:43.520 --> 00:14:45.350
And we're applying
transformations

00:14:45.350 --> 00:14:48.920
across the entire column in
these first three columns.

00:14:48.920 --> 00:14:52.530
Again, our goal here is to
reduce the risk of the data.

00:14:52.530 --> 00:14:54.680
So we are manipulating
and transforming

00:14:54.680 --> 00:14:57.170
the sensitive information
and replacing it

00:14:57.170 --> 00:14:59.390
with something
that is obfuscated.

00:14:59.390 --> 00:15:01.400
Now, in that fourth
column, we're

00:15:01.400 --> 00:15:04.940
combining the power of
DLP's inspection capability

00:15:04.940 --> 00:15:07.310
with the masking so
that we can actually

00:15:07.310 --> 00:15:10.220
go into these unstructured
comment fields,

00:15:10.220 --> 00:15:12.650
find the sensitive
element, and only mask

00:15:12.650 --> 00:15:13.963
that sensitive element.

00:15:13.963 --> 00:15:15.380
And this is something
that you can

00:15:15.380 --> 00:15:17.600
do because we've
combined that inspection

00:15:17.600 --> 00:15:22.280
capability with the data-masking
capability all in one platform.

00:15:22.280 --> 00:15:24.170
Otherwise, you'd have
to potentially redact

00:15:24.170 --> 00:15:26.390
the entire Comment Field column.

00:15:26.390 --> 00:15:27.920
But instead, you
can actually go in

00:15:27.920 --> 00:15:30.575
and redact only the sensitive
information in line.

00:15:33.860 --> 00:15:35.360
Now, another question
is, how can I

00:15:35.360 --> 00:15:37.580
improve protection
and process my data

00:15:37.580 --> 00:15:39.320
in a confidential manner?

00:15:39.320 --> 00:15:40.880
Again, now that
you have visibility

00:15:40.880 --> 00:15:43.410
into where your
sensitive data exists,

00:15:43.410 --> 00:15:46.080
you can use
confidential processing

00:15:46.080 --> 00:15:48.330
to process that more securely.

00:15:48.330 --> 00:15:50.580
An example of that would
be Confidential Dataproc,

00:15:50.580 --> 00:15:53.940
which offers encryption
in use, which can easily

00:15:53.940 --> 00:15:57.750
add security protections to your
most sensitive data workloads.

00:15:57.750 --> 00:16:01.650
Here's an example where we are
turning on confidential compute

00:16:01.650 --> 00:16:05.610
with a simple command-line
flag as we create and launch

00:16:05.610 --> 00:16:07.273
our Dataproc pipeline.

00:16:10.730 --> 00:16:13.850
Now, Confidential
Dataproc is now in GA.

00:16:13.850 --> 00:16:17.810
Now, Dataproc is a fully
managed and highly scalable

00:16:17.810 --> 00:16:20.810
service for running things
like Apache Spark, Flink,

00:16:20.810 --> 00:16:22.220
and Presto.

00:16:22.220 --> 00:16:26.240
And Confidential Dataproc adds
that data encryption in use,

00:16:26.240 --> 00:16:29.270
again, to protect your
most sensitive data.

00:16:29.270 --> 00:16:32.450
And it does this by using
confidential VMs to provide

00:16:32.450 --> 00:16:35.540
that in-line memory encryption.

00:16:35.540 --> 00:16:39.460
And this is powered by AMD's
secure encrypted virtualization

00:16:39.460 --> 00:16:40.300
processors.

00:16:43.057 --> 00:16:45.390
And this brings us to the end
of our talk on how to take

00:16:45.390 --> 00:16:47.020
charge of your sensitive data.

00:16:47.020 --> 00:16:50.520
And we'll leave you with a few
key takeaways and action items.

00:16:50.520 --> 00:16:54.960
First, automatic DLP is
now available for BigQuery

00:16:54.960 --> 00:16:57.570
and can give you insight
into your entire BigQuery

00:16:57.570 --> 00:16:59.610
footprint across your org.

00:16:59.610 --> 00:17:02.880
You can get started in the
Cloud Console by navigating

00:17:02.880 --> 00:17:05.940
to Security and then
Data Loss Prevention,

00:17:05.940 --> 00:17:07.650
or you can read
our documentation

00:17:07.650 --> 00:17:11.829
at cloud.google.com/dlp.

00:17:11.829 --> 00:17:14.890
You can also check out
obfuscation and masking,

00:17:14.890 --> 00:17:17.560
or what we call
de-identification,

00:17:17.560 --> 00:17:21.250
also at cloud.google.com/dlp.

00:17:21.250 --> 00:17:23.950
And you can read more about
confidential processing,

00:17:23.950 --> 00:17:27.220
which is available
with VMs, GKE,

00:17:27.220 --> 00:17:30.925
and now in GA for Dataproc at
cloud.google.com/confidential

00:17:30.925 --> 00:17:33.940
computing.

00:17:33.940 --> 00:17:35.760
Thank you again for
joining us today,

00:17:35.760 --> 00:17:37.510
and we're happy to be
part of your journey

00:17:37.510 --> 00:17:39.920
as you take care of
your sensitive data.

00:17:39.920 --> 00:17:41.610
Thank you.

